    "cardQuote": "I feel sad for ownCloud and Nextcloud. ownCloud has a brand and this fork dilutes it. It will be on everyone's mind especially since the founder has left to create a competitor.",
    "quote": "I feel sad for ownCloud and Nextcloud. ownCloud has a brand and this fork dilutes it. It will be on everyone's mind especially since the founder has left to create a competitor. For Nextcloud, unless they can explicitly state why this fork exists, it's hard to side with them. What kind of ideological differences are we talking about? Sure, you don't need to throw around personal accusations or finger point but it would help to know what motivated this. On a related note, this is a risk which many platform companies take. If you opensource your project completely, then anyone can fork it and take it ahead if the forker is a big or well known person. I think we got really lucky with iojs and node.",
    "storyTitle": "Owncloud has been forked into Nextcloud",
    "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=11823959",
    "outcome": "Became the most widely deployed open-source content collaboration platform, used by EU governments.",
    "outcomeDetail": "Nextcloud took most of ownCloud's contributors and quickly won public-sector and enterprise deployments. The European Commission described it as the most widely deployed open-source content collaboration platform, and Germany, France, Sweden, and the Netherlands adopted it.",
    "note": "The brand worry was backwards: ownCloud Inc. collapsed within hours of the fork, while Nextcloud became the brand that mattered. Forking was the rescue, not the dilution.",
